# Markets fail — so the state should step in?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why is a market failure not enough to justify intervention? What is the \"nirvana fallacy\"?"
next: {"title":"If you can't fix every distortion, should you fix one?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/53-if-you-can-t-fix-every-distortion-should.cast"}

## A cure that missed
sources:
    - id: davis2008
      title: The Effect of Driving Restrictions on Air Quality in Mexico City
      authors: Lucas W. Davis
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Hoy No Circula (from 20 November 1989) banned most cars one weekday by the last plate digit (5 or 6: Mondays), 460,000 vehicles a day. Across pollutants no evidence of improved air quality; registered vehicles rose by about 325,000, overwhelmingly used vehicles. WHO ozone guideline exceeded on 92% of days 1986–2005. Journal of Political Economy 116(1): 38–81.'

