    card title "A cure that missed"

When a market fails, should the state step in? It sounds obvious. Let's test it in  (@draw city@)Mexico City, in 1989.
    draw road car1 car2 car3 car4 car5 parallel true

B: The air was dreadful. Between 1986 and 2005, ozone broke the World He (@draw ozone@)alth Organization's limit on ninety-two percent of days.
    draw smog

That's a textbook mar (@draw fail@)ket failure. Every driver adds  (@flow road@)to the smog, and nobody pays for the share they add.

B: So the city stepped in. In Nove (@erase ozone@)mber 1989 it launched Hoy No Circula, roughly 'today it doesn't drive'.
    draw hnc

Each car stays home one weekday, picked by the last digit of its plate. Plates ending i (@highlight plate_t@)n five or six: no driving on Mondays.
    draw plate plate_t

B: That took four hundred and s (@draw off@)ixty thousand cars off the road every weekday. About one in five.
    fade car5 to 0.15

So surely the air got cleaner.
    highlight smog

    quiz "Your guess: what happened to Mexico City's air?" judge false voice b
        right: Here is what the monitoring stations showed.
        * Clearly cleaner
        * A little cleaner
        * No measurable change
        * Dirtier

B: Lucas Davis checked the hourly readings from the city's monitoring stations, before and after. He  (@draw stamp@)found no evidence the air got cleaner, for any of the pollutants.
    highlight smog

Why not? Because a family with two cars, plates ending in different digits, can drive every day.
    highlight plate_t

B: Registered vehicles  (@draw used1 used2 used3 used4 parallel true@)rose by around three hundred and twenty-five tho (@draw more@)usand, mostly used cars from elsewhere, and older cars pollute more.
    erase plate plate_t hnc off

So the market failure  (@draw tick@)was real, and the state re (@draw fix@)ally stepped in. The cure  (@draw cross@)still missed.

Pointing at a failure, it turns out, is only half an argument.
    highlight fail tick effect underline

## Real against real
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 3.6
    'y':
      - 0
      - 115
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 170
      w: 620
      h: 470
vars:
    running:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 10
    mistakes:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 40
    council:
      expr: 100 - running - mistakes
sources:
    - id: demsetz1969
      title: 'Information and Efficiency: Another Viewpoint'
      authors: Harold Demsetz
      year: 1969
      finding: 'Contrasts the ''nirvana approach'' (real institutions vs an ideal norm) with the ''comparative institution approach'' (choice between real alternative arrangements); those who adopt the nirvana viewpoint ''seek to discover discrepancies between the ideal and the real''. Journal of Law and Economics 12(1): 1–22. A critique of Arrow (1962) on invention.'

    axes ax x_label "" y_label "Net gain to the town" ticks.y [0,50,100] hidden true
    curve base #3d3833 expr "0" hidden true
    curve ideal_top dashed #8f887c expr "100" x_from 0.35 x_to 1.05 hidden true
    region ideal_bar x_from 0.35 x_to 1.05 between ["ideal_top","base"] style.fill #e7e2d8 style.opacity 0.6 hidden true
    text ideal_v "100" #8f887c at.data [0.7,107] font_size 30 hidden true
    text lab_ideal "Ideal" #8f887c at.data [0.7,-9] font_size 28 hidden true
    curve market_top #2f6b8f expr "70" x_from 1.35 x_to 2.05 hidden true
    region market_bar x_from 1.35 x_to 2.05 between ["market_top","base"] style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.35 hidden true
    text lab_market "Market" #2f6b8f at.data [1.7,-9] font_size 28 hidden true
    curve mline dashed #2f6b8f expr "70" x_from 0.2 x_to 3.55 hidden true
    text market_v "70" #2f6b8f at.data [1.7,62] font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow gapar #b5482e from.data [1.7,72] to.data [1.7,99] hidden true
    text gap_t "Failure: 30" #b5482e at.data [2.38,84] font_size 28 hidden true
    arrow stepin #3d3833 from.data [1.45,76] to.data [1.08,96] hidden true
    text stepin_t "Step in!" at.data [1.45,108] font_size 28 hidden true
    text quote "\"seek to discover\ndiscrepancies between\nthe ideal and the real\"" #3d3833 cites ["demsetz1969"] x 830 y 390 font_size 30 style.italic true hidden true
    mark strike around stepin stepin_t kind cross hidden true
    curve council_top #b5482e expr "100 - running - mistakes" x_from 2.75 x_to 3.45 hidden true
    region council_bar x_from 2.75 x_to 3.45 between ["council_top","base"] style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.35 hidden true
    text council_v "{council:0}" #b5482e at.data [3.1,-21] font_size 30 hidden true
    text lab_council "Real council" #b5482e at.data [3.1,-9] font_size 28 hidden true
    text run_t "Running: −10" #b5482e x 845 y 520 font_size 30 hidden true
    text mis_t "Mistakes: −{mistakes:0}" #b5482e x 845 y 460 font_size 30 hidden true
    text rvr "Real vs real" #3d3833 x 845 y 350 font_size 40 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch calc center work [{"tex":"\\text{Step in only if } \\text{gain}_{\\,state} > \\text{gain}_{\\,market}"},{"tex":"\\text{gain}_{\\,state} = \\text{Ideal} - \\text{running} - \\text{mistakes}"},{"tex":"100 - 10 - 25 = 65 < 70"}] font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Real against real"

Mexico City's fix failed even though the failure was real. To see where the argument slips, give Harbourtown's fishing grounds some numbers, illustrative ones.
    draw ax base

B: Say the grounds, managed perfectly, are worth a hundred a year to the town. That's the ideal.
    draw ideal_top ideal_bar ideal_v lab_ideal

And left alone, every boat fishes a bit too (@draw mline@) hard, and the grounds give seventy.
    draw market_top market_bar market_v lab_market

B: A gap of thirty. That's the market failure.
    draw gapar gap_t

So the harbour council should step in and close the gap?
    draw stepin stepin_t

B: That's the move Harold Demsetz attacked in 1969. He cal (@draw quote@)led it the nirvana approach: compare the real market w (@highlight ideal_bar market_bar effect box@)ith an ideal, find a gap, and blame the market.
    text dname "Harold Demsetz, 1969" #3d3833 cites ["demsetz1969"] x 830 y 500 font_size 32

But (@draw strike@) the ideal isn't on the menu. Nobody can vote for it.

B: Ri (@erase dname quote@)ght. What's on the menu is a real council. Start from the ideal and subtract what a real one costs.
    draw council_top council_bar lab_council council_v

Ten a year  (@draw run_t@)to run it: inspectors, paperwork, enforcement.
    animate running 10 duration 2 ghost true

B: And its mistakes: a quota se (@draw mis_t@)t too high or too low, the wrong boats licensed. Say twenty-five.
    animate mistakes 25 duration 2.5

Sixt (@erase gapar gap_t stepin stepin_t strike@)y-five. That's below the market's seventy, so here, leaving it alone wins.
    highlight council_bar mline

B: That's Demsetz's rule: compare real with real.
    draw rvr

B: Try it yourself: drag the council's mistakes down, and find where stepping in starts to pay.
    explore params ["mistakes"]

    erase rvr

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: There's a one-line rule behind this.

@math

    erase mathq

    fade ax base ideal_top ideal_bar ideal_v lab_ideal market_top market_bar lab_market mline market_v council_top council_bar council_v lab_council run_t mis_t to 0.15

Step in only when the real state's gain beats the real market's.
    draw calc_box calc_line_1

B: And the real state's gain is the ideal, minus running costs, minus mistakes.
    draw calc_line_2

Here, a hundred minus ten minus twenty-five is sixty-five, short of seventy.
    draw calc_line_3

    erase calc

@after

    erase mathq

    fade ax base ideal_top ideal_bar ideal_v lab_ideal market_top market_bar lab_market mline market_v council_top council_bar council_v lab_council run_t mis_t to 1

## Both ways round
sources:
    - id: leape2006
      title: The London Congestion Charge
      authors: Jonathan Leape
      year: 2006
      finding: 'The charge introduced in February 2003 cut traffic delays inside the zone by about 30% in its first year. Journal of Economic Perspectives 20(4): 157–176.'
    - id: davis2008
      title: The Effect of Driving Restrictions on Air Quality in Mexico City
      authors: Lucas W. Davis
      year: 2008
      finding: 'No evidence that Hoy No Circula improved air quality, across pollutants. Journal of Political Economy 116(1): 38–81.'

    text hdr_m "Market" #2f6b8f x 310 y 655 font_size 34 hidden true
    text hdr_s "State" #b5482e x 790 y 655 font_size 34 hidden true
    text row_i "Ideal" #8f887c x 80 y 565 font_size 34 hidden true
    text row_r "Real" #3d3833 x 80 y 335 font_size 34 hidden true
    box im "Ideal market" dashed #8f887c x 310 y 565 width 280 height 120 radius 14 font_size 34 style.opacity 0.6 style.stroke #8f887c hidden true
    box is "Ideal state" dashed #8f887c x 790 y 565 width 280 height 120 radius 14 font_size 34 style.opacity 0.6 style.stroke #8f887c hidden true
    box rm "Real market" #2f6b8f x 310 y 335 width 280 height 120 radius 14 icon shop icon_side left font_size 34 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    box rs "Real state" #b5482e x 790 y 335 width 280 height 120 radius 14 icon "classical building" icon_side left font_size 34 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    arrow diag1 #3d3833 from.anchor top_right from.ref rm to.anchor bottom_left to.ref is hidden true
    text diag1_t "Nirvana fallacy" #3d3833 x 790 y 450 font_size 32 hidden true
    arrow diag2 #3d3833 from.anchor top_left from.ref rs to.anchor bottom_right to.ref im hidden true
    text diag2_t "Same, backwards" #3d3833 x 310 y 450 font_size 32 hidden true
    text london "London charge, 2003:\ndelays −30% ✓" #4f8a3a cites ["leape2006"] x 870 y 215 font_size 28 hidden true
    text mexico "Mexico City ban:\nair no better ✗" #b5482e cites ["davis2008"] x 630 y 215 font_size 28 hidden true
    arrow cmp #3d3833 from.anchor right from.ref rm to.anchor left to.ref rs style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text cmp_t "Compare\nthese" #3d3833 x 550 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    text cons "Real vs real:\nconsensus" #3d3833 x 310 y 215 font_size 32 hidden true
    text cont "Which wins:\ncontested" #b5482e x 790 y 215 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Both ways round"

Real against real, the council lost. Put the whole comparison on one grid: market or state, ideal or real.
    draw hdr_m hdr_s row_i row_r im is rm rs

The nirvana fallacy runs along this diagonal: a real market against an ideal state.
    draw diag1 diag1_t

B: But hold on. Doesn't that hand every lobby an excuse? Propose any rule, and someone says the government might fail.
    highlight is

It would, if you only ran it one way. Comparin (@draw diag2 diag2_t@)g a real state with a perfect mark (@highlight im@)et is the same mistake, backwards.

B: And real states do win sometimes. In 2003 London began charging drivers (@draw london@) to enter its centre, and in the first year delays there fell by about thirty percent.
    highlight rs

While (@draw mexico@) Mexico City's ban left the air no cleaner. The same kind of probl (@highlight london mexico@)em, two real states, opposite results.

B: So the honest compa (@fade im is row_i to 0.25@)rison is this (@draw cmp cmp_t@) bottom row, one case at a time.
    erase diag1 diag1_t diag2 diag2_t

How sure are we? That you must compare (@draw cons@) real with real is close to consensus.
    erase london mexico

B: Which side (@draw cont@) wins in a given case is contested, and it's settled (@highlight cont effect underline@) by evidence like Davis's, not by slogans.

    quiz "Too few taxis at night. A councillor: 'The market fails, so the council must set taxi numbers.' What does the argument skip?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: How a real rule would do: a real council can set the number wrong too, so its rule has to beat the real taxi market.
        wrong: Grant the shortage. What else has to be true for the rule to help? Here, how a real taxi rule would do.
        * Proof that taxis are scarce
        + How a real taxi rule would do
        * Whether drivers want more work

    quiz "Say private broadband reaches 7 homes in 10. A council network promises all 10; real ones elsewhere reached about 8, late. What should the town compare?"
        right: 7 against a real council network: about 8, minus its delays and cost overruns. The promised 10 is the ideal, not the menu.
        wrong: Which of those numbers could the town actually get? Here, 7 against what a real council network would deliver.
        * 7 against the promised 10
        + 7 against what a real council network would deliver
        * Whichever network is faster

    quiz "'Governments are slow and get captured, so leave vaccination entirely to the market.' What is the flaw?" voice b
        right: It compares a real state with a perfect market: real vaccine markets have their own failures, so the fair test is real against real.
        wrong: Is the market it imagines one that really exists? Here, it compares a real state with a perfect market.
        * Vaccines are not sold in markets
        + It compares a real state with a perfect market
        * Governments are never slow

So, does a market failure mean the state should step in? Only if the state we would really get beats the market we really have, and that turns on what a state can know, and what the people running it want.
    highlight rm rs cmp

