      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1us5csu'
    '|b||Three new cases. Too few taxis at night. A councillor: ''The market fails, so the council must set taxi numbers.'' What does the argument skip?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8520
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:ehqztz'
    '|b||How a real rule would do: a real council can set the number wrong too, so its rule has to beat the real taxi market.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7400
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||Say private broadband reaches 7 homes in 10. A council network promises all 10; real ones elsewhere reached about 8, late. What should the town compare?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11960
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||7 against a real council network: about 8, minus its delays and cost overruns. The promised 10 is the ideal, not the menu.':
